			<article class="prose">
<h2>On why the closing line is one word long</h2>
<p>The last line of this book is one word long.</p>
<p><em>Home.</em></p>
<p>I am going to explain why, and I want to be clear that the explanation is not the point. The point is the word. But you have bought the book, and readers are allowed to ask, so:</p>
<h2>The problem with endings</h2>
<p>Almost every novel I love ends badly. Not badly-written — <em>badly</em>. It keeps talking. The last chapter cannot stop. It explains the theme, then restates the theme, then has a character look back over the year and say the word <em>family</em> in a slightly different key. It says <em>and they were happy</em> when the whole book has already made you believe it, and in doing so it makes you distrust the ninety thousand words that came before.</p>
<p>I have read four hundred books that ended that way. It is the single most common failure of the form, and I did not want to be in the club.</p>
<h2>What the recursion demands</h2>
<p>This novel is built on a grammatical loop. <em>The kind of</em> turns back on itself, and it does that two thousand seven hundred sixteen times, and the loop is the argument: a life that keeps describing itself instead of arriving anywhere. Every sentence is a door that opens onto another door.</p>
<p>A loop cannot be closed by another loop. If the last sentence is also a <em>the kind of</em>, the book ends by refusing to end. So the ending has to break the pattern — and the smallest possible break is the loudest possible statement.</p>
<h2>What the word has to do</h2>
<p>It has to do three things.</p>
<p><strong>It has to be the book's own word, not the theme's.</strong> <em>Home</em> is not a summary. It is not love, not grief, not growth, not redemption. It is the plainest noun in the English language and it is the one thing in this entire book that nobody argues about. Everyone in this story is fighting about the piece, about the money, about the name on the door, about what it means to be wanted. Nobody fights about home. That is why it is the one word in the book that needs no explanation.</p>
<p><strong>It has to be short enough to carry.</strong> I could have written <em>and he was home and she was home and for a long time neither of them said anything.</em> It is a better sentence than <em>Home.</em> It is also a worse ending, because it hands the reader a paragraph to interpret instead of a word to hold. You can carry one word out of a room. You can hold it in your mouth for a year. A paragraph has to be re-read.</p>
<p><strong>It has to be said by her, and only by her.</strong> The word belongs to June. It is hers, it is a word she has earned, and it is the only thing in the book that is not argued over, not negotiated, not performed for an audience. It is one person, alone, knowing where she is.</p>
<h2>The last four hundred words I deleted</h2>
<p>They were good. That is the part nobody tells you about the ones you delete — they are good. I wrote a paragraph about the cello on the stand in the hallway, uncovered, for the first time in the book. I wrote a paragraph about the studio in the morning, the light, the two of them not speaking and the not-speaking being the best thing that had ever happened to either of them. I wrote a paragraph where he says her name the way you say a thing you are holding.</p>
<p>I deleted all of it, because every one of those paragraphs is a place for the reader to stop. And the reader should not be able to stop. They should reach the last line and find it already finished, and find themselves holding the whole book in one word, and find that the word has been standing there the entire time, in the book, in the house, in the door, in the name of the place where she is — waiting.</p>
<p>That is what a coda is for. Not a summary. A <em>door</em>.</p>
<h2>Why the word is in italics</h2>
<p>Because the book is not saying it. The book is not arguing, not explaining, not performing. The book is <em>quoting her</em> — setting down, in the only typographic voice it has, the one sentence in the entire novel that no one says out loud and no one has to.</p>
<h2>The thing I will not say</h2>
<p>There is a reason the last word is not <em>finishing</em>.</p>
<p><em>Finishing</em> is the held-verb. It is the thing the whole book is built around, and it is released in chapter fourteen, in the middle, structurally, where it can do the most work. It has been earned by then. The ending does not get to have it, because the ending is not about the piece anymore. The piece is done. The ending is about the door.</p>
<p>I did consider ending on <em>finishing</em>. I want to be honest about that, because it would have been the showier choice and several people who love the book asked for it.</p>
<p>I decided against it for one reason: it would make the last word about <em>the work</em>. And this book is not about the work.</p>
<p>It is about a man who could not stop composing, and a woman who could not stop waiting, and a house in which a cello stood uncovered in a hallway for a long time — and the particular, specific, unrepeatable afternoon when neither of them was going to get the piece back.</p>
<p><em>Home.</em></p>
<p><em>— E.L.F.</em></p>
